    <p class="plate-note">Clausius named it in 1865; Boltzmann explained it in 1877 with the equation now
    cut on his gravestone in Vienna. A macrostate &mdash; "warm", "spread out", "mixed" &mdash; is a
    description satisfied by some number <em>W</em> of microstates, and entropy is the logarithm of that
    number. The second law is then nearly a tautology with teeth: a system wanders into the macrostates
    that have more ways of being realised, because there are more of them. Nothing pushes it there.
    <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> established science.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Arrow <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Why Time Has a Direction</h3>
      <p>Every fundamental law except one is reversible: run the film of two colliding particles backwards
      and it is still legal physics. The second law is the exception, and it is not a separate law of
      nature so much as a statement about initial conditions. The universe began in an extraordinarily
      low-entropy state &mdash; the <em>past hypothesis</em> &mdash; and everything that has happened since
      is the enormous relaxation out of it.</p>
      <p>The arrow of time in a broken cup, in a memory, in ageing and in the fact that you know the past
      and not the future are all the same arrow, and it points away from that beginning. Why the beginning
      was so ordered is a genuinely open question. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> the law.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> the explanation of the initial state.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Information <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Landauer's Limit, and the Demon</h3>
      <p>Maxwell imagined a demon sorting fast molecules from slow and so beating the second law. The
      resolution took a century and is the most beautiful result in the subject: Landauer showed in 1961
      that <em>erasing</em> one bit must dissipate at least <em>kT</em>&nbsp;ln&nbsp;2 of heat, and Bennett
      pointed out in 1982 that the demon's memory eventually fills and must be cleared. The books balance
      exactly.</p>
      <p>Information is therefore physical, and entropy in Shannon's sense and entropy in Clausius's are
      the same quantity in different units. B&eacute;rut and colleagues measured Landauer's bound directly
      in 2012. Any claim that reality keeps an account of itself has to start here &mdash; because here it
      demonstrably does, and the account has a price in joules.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Phases <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>What a Phase of Matter Is</h3>
      <p>Not a kind of stuff but a kind of <em>order</em>. Solid, liquid, gas, plasma; and beyond the
      schoolroom four: supercritical fluids, superfluids, superconductors, Bose&ndash;Einstein condensates,
      liquid crystals, quark-gluon plasma, and the topological phases Kosterlitz and Thouless were given
      the 2016 Nobel for.</p>
      <p>Landau's insight is the one that matters here: a phase transition is a change of
      <em>symmetry</em>. A liquid looks the same from everywhere; a crystal does not, so freezing breaks a
      symmetry and an order parameter rises from zero. At the critical point, correlations reach every
      length scale at once and utterly different substances &mdash; a magnet, a fluid, an alloy &mdash;
      obey the same numbers. Wilson's renormalisation group explained why, and those critical exponents
      are measured to several decimal places.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Charge <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Where Electromagnetism Comes From</h3>
      <p>Charge is conserved, quantised, and comes in exactly two signs. The proton's charge and the
      electron's are equal and opposite to better than one part in 10<sup>21</sup>, which is why bulk
      matter is neutral and why you are not torn apart by the electrostatic force &mdash; which is some
      10<sup>36</sup> times stronger than gravity between the same two particles.</p>
      <p>In the standard model that conservation is not an accident: it follows by Noether's theorem from
      a symmetry, the U(1) gauge symmetry, and the force itself is carried by the photon. Its strength is
      fixed by the fine structure constant, about 1/137, which is measured to twelve digits and is not
      known to be derivable from anything.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

