  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Teaching <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Why These Four</h3>
      <p>The order holds that there are further poles beyond the pair that makes an atom, and that they
      stand at <strong>101</strong> and at <strong>666</strong> when the ascent is counted numerically, the
      counting governed by the primes &mdash; because the primes are where a number cannot be integrated
      into smaller factors, and so are exactly the places where an integration must be made rather than
      assembled.</p>
      <p>101 is the first gate: the smallest pole at which a thing is no longer one and not yet many, a
      one, a nothing and a one. 666 is the second: the pole of material completion, where everything that
      can be gathered has been gathered. 999 is not a pole but a <em>face</em>. The being does not pass it
      on the first approach; it strikes or is deflected, returns to 666, and falls back to 333, where it
      reverberates &mdash; and goes on reverberating until it is finally able to surpass 999 and continue
      towards infinity. Alpha at the foot, omega at the head, and the whole climb observed.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Arithmetic, Checked <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>What Is Actually True About These Numbers</h3>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>101 is prime</strong> &mdash; and palindromic, the 26th prime, and the smallest prime with exactly three digits in base ten.</li>
        <li><strong>666 is not prime.</strong> It is 2 &times; 3<sup>2</sup> &times; 37. It <em>is</em> the 36th triangular number, the sum of every whole number from 1 to 36.</li>
        <li><strong>666 is the sum of the squares of the first seven primes</strong>: 4 + 9 + 25 + 49 + 121 + 169 + 289. That identity is real, and the order makes a great deal of it.</li>
        <li><strong>333, 666 and 999 are 9 &times; 37, 18 &times; 37 and 27 &times; 37.</strong> The 37 is the whole trick: 111 = 3 &times; 37, so every repeated digit in base ten carries it.</li>
        <li><strong>And that last fact is the warning.</strong> Those relations are facts about writing numbers in base ten, not facts about the world. In base twelve, 666 is 476 and the pattern vanishes.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Where 666 Comes From <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>The Scholarship, Which Is Worth Having</h3>
      <p>Revelation 13:18 gives the number of the beast and invites the reader to calculate it. The
      overwhelming scholarly reading is gematria for <em>Neron Kesar</em> in Hebrew letters &mdash; a coded
      name for a living emperor, written under persecution. The strongest evidence is textual: some
      manuscripts read <strong>616</strong> instead, including Papyrus 115, and 616 is what you get from
      the Latin spelling <em>Nero</em> rather than the Greek.</p>
      <p>So the number was a name, and the name was a man who was burning people. Whatever any tradition
      has built on it since &mdash; and much has been built &mdash; that is where it started.
      <span class="hist">&#9670;</span> scholarship and history.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Standing Warning <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Eddington, 137, and the Lesson</h3>
      <p>Arthur Eddington &mdash; a great astrophysicist, the man who weighed the deflection of starlight
      in 1919 &mdash; became convinced that the fine structure constant was exactly 1/136, then exactly
      1/137 when the measurement moved, and built an elaborate numerology to prove it. It is about
      1/137.035999, it is not a whole number, and the episode is remembered as a caution rather than a
      result.</p>
      <p>Numbers that recur are the easiest thing in the world to find and the hardest thing to justify.
      The order states its own numerical doctrine knowing exactly this, and files it as myth for exactly
      this reason.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

